description:
Found by the oculist-d5c reviewer on 2026-09-02, adjacent to that bead but not attributable to it. Not fixed under d5c because it is a different failure family.

THE SITE: test('the absorption flash element is removed once its animation finishes (no leak)') at test/trail_effect.test.js:655 does

  await replay();
  await page.waitForSelector(FLASH, { timeout: POLL_TIMEOUT });
  await page.waitForFunction(() => document.querySelectorAll('.oc-beacon').length === 0, ...);

THE SHAPE, and why it is NOT the d5c family: d5c was about a read happening AFTER a successful wait, in a Node-side gap. There is no such read here, so d5c's fix does not apply and did not touch this line. The risk here is one step earlier: the flash is transient and self-cleans, so it can mount and disappear entirely between poll ticks of waitForSelector, which then never observes it and times out at 5000ms.

WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN THE RAW FLAKE RATE SUGGESTS: a 5000ms timeout is exactly the signature of the --test-concurrency=4 CONTENTION family (see the test-suite-flake memory, key test-suite-flake-npm-test-runs-at-test). So when this fires it will be triaged as known contention noise and dismissed, even though it is a test defect with a real fix. That misattribution is the actual cost. The d5c investigation showed how expensive this confusion is: the two families were conflated for five beads before the fast-vs-5000ms discriminator separated them.

Note the second wait is the one carrying the test's meaning (the flash is cleaned up). The first wait exists only to prove the flash rendered at all, so a fix must keep that proof rather than delete the line.

DONE WHEN: the test no longer depends on waitForSelector catching a short-lived element in the act. Likely direction: a single page-side observer armed BEFORE replay() fires, which records that the flash appeared and then that it went away, so the proof does not depend on sampling at the right instant. Whatever the mechanism, the test must still fail if the flash never renders AND still fail if it renders and is never removed. Verify both with mutations against extension/content.js, then revert them.

Not urgent: no observed failure at this site yet, only the mechanism. Filed so the next 5000ms timeout in this file is not automatically written off as contention.
